course: money
lesson: 40
title: Gambling and honesty with change
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Gambling means betting money on something you cannot control, like horses, scratchcards or a claw machine, and the companies that run it are set up so that, on average, players lose more than they win.
explain: Picture Uncle Mick betting £20 every Saturday: over 52 weeks that is £1,040, and if he wins £200 back he has still lost £840; "I'll win big one day" is exactly the feeling gambling feeds on.
explain: Honesty is the other side of money: when the shop gives Mia £5 change instead of 50p, she hands back the extra £4.50, because the shop lady might have to pay it out of her own wages; Gran does the same when a stall undercharges her by £2.
explain: This is the last lesson of the money course, and everything comes together: money works because people trust each other, so a fair price, an honest till, a debt paid back and a found purse returned keep that trust alive.
explain: Earn, save, spend wisely, give when you can, ask "says who?" before you believe a promise, and remember that money matters but never measures how good a person is.
fact: Gambling is betting money on something uncertain; a bookmaker is a business that takes bets, and on average players lose more than they win.
fact: £20 a week for 52 weeks is £1,040; winning £200 back still leaves £1,040 − £200 = £840 lost.
fact: Odds are the chance of winning; the top prize on a scratchcard or lottery might be 1 in several million, so most people lose most of the time.
fact: Gambling can become an addiction, which means not being able to stop doing something harmful; it can start at £5 a week and grow to £500, and it hurts whole families.
fact: Betting companies pay for many adverts, like 10 in one match; some want fewer gambling ads, especially where children watch, while others say adults should choose freely.
fact: A fair coin has a 1 in 2 chance on each flip, and 5 heads in a row has a 1 in 32 chance; each flip is fresh, so a streak does not make the next result "due".
fact: Claw machines are set so that you usually lose: £5 spent on a £2 teddy is £3 lost even if you win it.
fact: Even small bets between friends make nothing overall: if one wins £1, the other loses £1, and rows often follow, so many families have a "no betting for money" rule for kids.
fact: Keeping extra change is not honest: getting £5 instead of 50p is £4.50 too much, and shop workers can lose money from their pay when tills don't add up.
fact: If a cash machine gives £60 instead of £40, the £20 extra is not yours; banks usually find such errors and take the money back anyway.
fact: Returning lost property, like a purse with £20 and a bank card, is honest and kind; imagine how you would feel if it were yours.
fact: Honesty works both ways: if a stall charges £6 instead of £8, paying the right £8 gives the trader back the £2 they earned.
fact: The whole money course rests on one idea: money works because people trust each other, and honesty keeps that trust going.
exercise: Mia should get 50p change but gets £5. How much too much is that? | £4.50 | £5.50 | 50p | £4
exercise: Why does Mia give the extra money back? | It isn't hers, and the shop worker might have to pay it | Keeping it would make her a millionaire | The shop always has to give children a little bit extra by law | Change can only be spent at that same shop
exercise: The cash machine gives Dad £60 instead of £40. How much extra is that? | £20 | £100 | £40 | £60
exercise: Uncle Mick bets £20 every week for 52 weeks. How much does he bet in a year? | £1,040 | £1,020 | £520 | £72
exercise: Uncle Mick bet £1,040 and won £200 back. How much has he lost? | £840 | £1,240 | £200 | £1,040
exercise: Why do people on average lose money gambling? | The betting companies are set up so players lose more than they win | Gambling is always fair, so everyone breaks even | Players who lose are just unlucky once | Winners must pay all the money back
exercise: Josh's mum buys a £2 scratchcard and wins £0. What are the odds of the top prize likely to be? | About 1 in several million | About 1 in 2 | About 1 in 10 | Certain if you buy two
exercise: Leo's dad says gambling "starts small, then it gets you." What is he describing? | Gambling addiction, which hurts whole families | A new hobby that always saves money in the end | A safe and steady way for grown-ups to earn a living | A game that only children are allowed to play online
exercise: Grace sees 10 betting ads in one football match. What do people argue about? | Some want fewer gambling ads where kids watch; others say adults should choose | Everyone agrees betting ads should be on all day | Nobody has any opinion about betting ads | Betting ads are only shown in cartoons
exercise: What is the chance of a fair coin landing heads 5 times in a row? | 1 in 32 | 1 in 5 | 1 in 10 | 1 in 2
exercise: Ruby's mate wins 5 coin flips in a row and Ruby yells "Rigged!" What is true? | A fair coin can do that sometimes; each flip is fresh | It is completely impossible, so the coin must be rigged | After five wins in a row, the next flip must be Ruby's | Coins always swap sides every single time they are flipped
exercise: Kai spends £5 on a claw machine for a £2 teddy. Even if he wins it, how much has he lost? | £3 | £5 | £2 | £7
exercise: Ella finds a purse with £20 and a bank card on the bus. What should she do? | Hand it to the driver so it can get back to its owner | Keep the £20 and throw away the card, because finders are keepers | Use the card to buy herself lunch | Leave it on the seat for the next person
exercise: Two friends bet £1 each. One wins £1 and one loses £1. How much has the group made? | £0 | £2 | £1 | −£2
exercise: The market stall charges Gran £6 instead of £8. How much was she undercharged? | £2 | £14 | £6 | £8
exercise: What does Gran do about the mistake in her favour? | Pays the right £8, because honesty works both ways | Keeps the £2, because it was the trader's own fault | Asks for another £2 off the price for all her trouble | Says nothing about it and goes on shopping there every day
test: Mia's mate whispers, "Leg it!" when the shop gives her £5 instead of 50p change. ("Leg it" means run away.) What's the right move and why? | Give back £4.50, because the shop worker might have to pay it | Run, because shops never notice wrong change | Keep it, because the shop's mistake is the shop's problem | Spend it fast before anyone finds out
test: Uncle Mick says, "I'll win big one day, mark my words." Auntie, from Liverpool, says, "He's lost a fortune, la, it's not boss." ("Boss" means good.) What's the truth? | On average gamblers lose; he bet £1,040 and won £200 | He'll definitely win big if he keeps going | Betting every week always pays off in the end | Auntie is wrong; gambling every week actually saves you money
test: Josh's mum sighs, "Mug's game," after her scratchcard wins £0. ("Mug's game" means a thing only a fool expects to win at.) Why does she say that? | The chance of a big win is tiny, so most people lose | Scratchcards always win if you scratch them slowly enough | She thinks the scratchcards are actually made out of old mugs | Scratchcards are free to buy, so nothing at all is ever lost
test: Ruby flips coins with her mate. After 5 heads in a row, her mate says, "Tails is due now, defo." ("Defo" means definitely.) Is he right? | No, each flip is fresh: still 1 in 2 | Yes, after 5 heads tails is guaranteed | Yes, tails is now 5 times more likely | No, the coin will land on its edge next
test: What would happen if Kai kept playing the claw machine until he won the £2 teddy, spending £12? | He'd have lost £10 overall, even with the teddy | He'd be £10 richer because he won | He'd break even, because winning cancels the cost | The arcade would refund him for trying
test: The cash machine gives Dad an extra £20 by mistake. His mate says, "Free money, innit, the bank's minted." ("Minted" means rich.) What does Dad do and why? | Tells the bank, because it's not his, and they'd find it anyway | Keeps it, because banks are rich | Spends it fast so the bank can't take it back | Takes out more to see if it happens again
test: In a game, a slot machine "always pays out if you keep going". What's wrong with that picture? | Real gambling is set up so players lose more than they win | Nothing, real slot machines always pay out in the end | Real slot machines pay everyone back on Fridays | Real slot machines only take pennies, so it's fine to keep on playing
test: Josh's mate says, "Bet you a quid I can eat 10 marshmallows — no, 12!" Mum says, "No betting, boys, just have fun." Why might she say that? | Even small money bets can cause rows, so a no-betting rule is common | Betting is the only way to make eating fun | Marshmallows cost more than a quid | Mum wants to win the quid herself
test: Gran at the market is charged £6 instead of £8. Her grandson, a Gen Alpha kid, says, "Gran, that's a W, just take it." ("A W" means a win.) Gran says, "You've made a mistake, love." What does Gran show? | Honesty works both ways, even when a mistake helps you | Gran can't count all the way to eight without making a mistake | She wants to pay a little extra as a tip for the trader | It's against the law to buy anything cheaper at street markets
test: Leo's dad says, quietly, "My own dad lost his house through gambling." What does this show about addiction? | It can grow from small bets until it hurts a whole family | Gambling only ever costs a few pounds | People who gamble always win their house back | Houses are often won at the bookmaker's
test: The last word of the course: your cousin says, "Money's just numbers, it doesn't matter if you're honest." What's the best answer? | Money only works because people trust each other, so honesty keeps it going | He's right, honesty and money have nothing to do with each other | Money works because people are afraid of the police | Money is just paper, so nothing about it matters
test: Ella hands in a purse with £20. Her friend says, "You could've had a takeaway, mate!" How would the owner feel, and what came next in the story? | Very relieved, and she sent Ella a thank-you card | Angry, and she asked Ella for £20 more | Nothing, because she never really wanted the purse back | Annoyed that Ella touched her purse
exam: It is given that change of fifty pence was due, whereas change of five pounds was tendered in error. Determine the excess sum which ought to be returned. | £4.50 | £5.50 | 50p | £4
exam: Ascertain the net loss of a person who stakes £20 weekly for fifty-two weeks and receives winnings of £200 in total. | £840 | £1,040 | £1,240 | £200
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the probability of a fair coin falling heads upon the sixth toss, five heads having previously been obtained? | One in two, each toss being independent of those preceding it | Certainty, a sixth head being inevitable | Impossibility, tails being obligatorily due | One in thirty-two, the preceding tosses being taken into account
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the foundation upon which the use of money rests. | The mutual trust of those who use it, which honesty preserves | The fear of punishment entertained by those who use it | The intrinsic value of the paper upon which notes are printed | The decree of any single shopkeeper concerning its worth
